schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: Quadratic voting cost curve (1 vote=1, 2 votes=4, 3 votes=9) reduces wealth domination
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    Under quadratic voting, casting n votes on one position costs n-squared
    units of influence (1 vote = 1 unit, 2 votes = 4 units, 3 votes = 9
    units), sharply increasing the cost of piling multiple votes behind one
    position, and Gitcoin uses this mechanism to allocate grant funding,
    balancing influence between large and small contributors.
  evidence: >-
    Article's own description of the quadratic-cost formula, attributed to
    Gitcoin's use of the mechanism for grant funding (cited to
    gitcoin.co/blog/quadratic-voting in the references).
  source_lineage: src/content/docs/voting-mechanisms.md
  interpretation: >-
    Used as the article's central worked example of manipulation-resistant
    voting math, showing concretely how the mechanism dampens (but the
    article notes does not eliminate) wealth-based dominance.
  uncertainty: >-
    The article itself qualifies the claim ("it's not perfect, it still
    advantages those with more resources") — no independent outcome data
    (e.g., measured shift in grant-allocation concentration) is cited beyond
    the mechanism's design math.
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/voting-mechanisms.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
